.TH "zelta\-rebase" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zelta rebase\f[R] \- rebase a dataset onto an upgraded upstream
while preserving local files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zelta rebase\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]upstream\f[R]
\f[I]target\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zelta rebase\f[R] replaces a production dataset with a new dataset
based on an upgraded upstream while preserving incremental backup
continuity and local files.
.PP
The old target is automatically renamed out of the way using its clone
origin snapshot name, and the new upstream is received at the original
target name.
If a preserve file is found, specified local files are copied from the
old target into the new one.
.PP
This is useful for image, template, appliance, and development workflows
where local changes must move forward onto a newer upstream base without
forcing a full backup history restart.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
\f[I]upstream\f[R]
Upgraded base dataset.
If a snapshot is specified (e.g., \f[I]pool/dataset\(atsnap\f[R]), that
snapshot is used; otherwise the latest snapshot is used.
.TP
\f[I]target\f[R]
Current production dataset to rebase.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rebase\-file\f[R] \f[I]file\f[R]
Override the default preserve file path.
If not specified, \f[B]zelta rebase\f[R] looks for
\f[I].zelta\-rebase.preserve\f[R] in the upstream dataset\(cqs
mountpoint.
.TP
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dryrun\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dry\-run\f[R]
Display underlying commands without executing them.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase verbosity.
Specify once for operational detail, twice for debug output.
.TP
\f[B]\-q\f[R], \f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Decrease log output.
.SH PRESERVE FILE
The upstream dataset may contain a \f[I].zelta\-rebase.preserve\f[R]
file listing paths relative to the dataset mountpoint to copy from the
old target into the new target.
.PP
Resolution order:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]\-\-rebase\-file\f[R] \f[I]file\f[R] (CLI override)
.IP "2." 3
\f[I].zelta\-rebase.preserve\f[R] in the upstream dataset\(cqs
mountpoint
.IP "3." 3
Neither found \(em no files are preserved; rebase behaves like a rotate
that preserves mountability
.SH EXAMPLES
Rebase a production dataset onto the latest upstream snapshot:
.IP
.EX
zelta rebase apool/jails/base cpool/jails/web01
.EE
.PP
Rebase onto a specific snapshot:
.IP
.EX
zelta rebase apool/jails/base\(atfreebsd14.2\-p1 cpool/jails/web01
.EE
.PP
Use an external preserve file:
.IP
.EX
zelta rebase \-\-rebase\-file /etc/jails/web01.preserve apool/jails/base cpool/jails/web01
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, non\-zero on error.
.SH NOTES
The old target is renamed to
\f[I]target\f[R]_\f[I]origin\-snapshot\f[R], where
\f[I]origin\-snapshot\f[R] is the name of the snapshot from which the
target was originally cloned.
.PP
Use \f[B]zelta match\f[R] before and after rebasing to confirm lineage
and backup state.
